{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And they shall take a bull, a son of cattle.</b> For the inauguration of their service. Another interpretation: <b>and they shall take a bull, a son of cattle</b> — because the firstborns rendered idolatrous service at the instruction of others, in the incident of the calf, as it is written: ״אלה אלהיך ישראל״, and we find that the public brings a bull for a burnt offering for a matter concealed from the congregation, as it is written: ״ואם מעיני העדה נעשתה לשגגה״ etc." "<b>And a second bull.</b> Rashi explained: what does Scripture come to teach by saying 'second'? That is to say, the sin-offering precedes the burnt offering — rather, it tells you: just as a burnt offering is not eaten, so this sin-offering is not eaten, for through it the hands of the Levites were filled, and they were consecrations like those explained in the section of Shemini." "<b>you shall take for a sin-offering.</b> It uses the term 'taking' and mentions the bull of the burnt offering first, in order to select the superior one for the burnt offering — for in any case, with regard to the order of offering, the sin-offering precedes the burnt offering. And a second bull you shall take <b>for a sin-offering</b>: Scripture gave the Levites an honor, that they did not bring a goat for a sin-offering, for had they done so, it would have appeared that it came for atonement for idolatrous worship, and they did not stumble in that."], :book :numbers, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 8, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:16:59.496932692Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}